      "side": 1,
      "message": "On the device I tested (UFS, 1GB load, 4MB chunks), depth 1/2/4/8 gave 284/252/252/252 ms. The load is storage bound from 2 chunks in flight, so a deeper queue does not help. A shallower one only throttles the load, and depth 1 still keeps 4MB in flight. For throttling, the chunk I/Os already carry the caller\u0027s ioprio and its blkcg, so ioprio or io.cost can limit it with finer control than a depth, right? If faster storage needs more I/O in flight, we can raise the depth in the ioctl. It costs no memory, and the caller would not know the right value either.\n\nAlso, this ioctl is a temporary solution for P27, not a general interface. I\u0027d like to keep its uAPI minimal.",
